' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' tar/source.mfb — the one seam every parser in this package reads bytes
' through.
'
' An archive is either a `List OF Byte` already in memory or an open
' `fs::File`. Everything above this file -- the header walk, the entry read,
' the extractor -- is written against `readAt` and `sizeOf` and never against
' either concrete source. That is what makes "the file form and the memory form
' produce identical results" true by construction rather than by two parsers
' that happen to agree today.
'
' The file branch never reads the whole file. `fs::readBytesAt` reads exactly
' the requested window and leaves the handle's read position where it found it
' (`mfb man fs readBytesAt`), so an `Archive` that aliases a caller's `fs::File`
' neither consumes it nor disturbs a reader sharing it. For tar this is what
' makes listing cheap: headers sit 512 bytes apart and the data between them is
' skipped by arithmetic, so opening a 40 GB archive reads one header per entry.
'
' This file is a deliberate copy of `packages/zip/src/source.mfb`, differing
' only in its error-message prefix. A shared third package for fifty lines would
' put an internal type in two packages' public surfaces and make each depend on
' a version of the other; the duplication is the cheaper cost.
'
' The length of a file is taken once, when the archive is opened, and kept. It
' is not asked for again on each read: a read is the hot path, and a listing
' makes several per entry. `readAt` still checks every window against it, and a
' file that shrinks after opening is caught there, as a short read.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

IMPORT fs
IMPORT collections
IMPORT errorCode

' Where an archive's bytes come from.
'
' The bytes of a memory archive are NOT held here. They sit in a plain field of
' `Archive`, and the union only says which kind of archive this is. The reason is
' cost, not tidiness: binding a union member by `MATCH` copies the member's
' contents, so a `List OF Byte` held in a member is copied on every read -- once
' per header, which makes opening an archive quadratic in its size. A list passed
' as a parameter is not copied, so the bytes are kept where they are passed from.
'
' Internal -- use `open`. The members are exported only because `Archive` has a
' field of this type, and an exported record's field type must be nameable by
' importers.
EXPORT TYPE MemorySource
END TYPE

' An archive whose bytes are in a file the caller has open.
'
' The handle belongs to the caller: this package never closes it. Closing it
' while an `Archive` still refers to it makes later reads fail with
' `errorCode::ErrResourceClosed`.
'
' The length is taken once, when the archive is opened, and kept. A read is the
' hot path, and a listing makes several per entry. `readAt` still checks every
' window against it, and a file that shrinks after opening is caught there, as a
' short read.
EXPORT TYPE FileSource
  PUBLIC handle AS RES fs::File
  PUBLIC length AS Integer
END TYPE

EXPORT UNION Source
  MemorySource
  FileSource
END UNION

' How many bytes the archive holds.
FUNC sizeOf(archive AS Archive) AS Integer
  MATCH archive.source
    CASE MemorySource
      RETURN len(archive.bytes)
    CASE FileSource(f)
      RETURN f.length
  END MATCH
END FUNC

' The `count` bytes at `offset`.
'
' Raises `errorCode::ErrInvalidFormat` when the window is not wholly inside the
' archive -- including the case where a file was truncated underneath an open
' handle, which shows up as a short read.
FUNC readAt(archive AS Archive, offset AS Integer, count AS Integer) AS List OF Byte
  IF offset < 0 OR count < 0 THEN
    FAIL error(errorCode::ErrInvalidFormat, "tar: read of " & toString(count) & " bytes at " & toString(offset) & " is out of range")
  END IF
  LET total AS Integer = sizeOf(archive)
  IF offset + count > total THEN
    FAIL error(errorCode::ErrInvalidFormat, "tar: read of " & toString(count) & " bytes at " & toString(offset) & " runs past the end of a " & toString(total) & "-byte archive")
  END IF
  MATCH archive.source
    CASE MemorySource
      RETURN collections::mid(archive.bytes, offset, count)
    CASE FileSource(f)
      LET got AS List OF Byte = fs::readBytesAt(f.handle, offset, count)
      IF len(got) <> count THEN
        FAIL error(errorCode::ErrInvalidFormat, "tar: file ended after " & toString(len(got)) & " of " & toString(count) & " bytes at " & toString(offset))
      END IF
      RETURN got
  END MATCH
END FUNC
